## Overview

Marathon uses multiple ammo types including ballistic Light Rounds and Heavy Rounds plus energy Volt Batteries, Volt Cells, and MIPS ammunition.{{cite:gamesradar-weapons}} CyberAcme stocks basic ammo in its armory offerings to help runners stay supplied.{{cite:gamesradar-factions}}

## Ballistic ammo

Light Rounds and Heavy Rounds are Marathon's ballistic ammo categories players must manage.{{cite:gamesradar-weapons}} Heavy ammo guns deal higher damage but trade off fire rate and increased recoil.{{cite:pcgamer-review}} Examples: the M77 uses Light Rounds and the Ares railgun uses Heavy Rounds.{{cite:gamesradar-weapons}}

## Energy ammo (Volt)

Volt weapons are powered by energy cartridges referred to as Volt Batteries or Volt Cells.{{cite:gamesradar-weapons}} Volt guns drain shields faster than ballistic weapons and can recharge over time.{{cite:pcgamer-review}} Reloading a Volt weapon before its energy fully depletes discards its remaining energy.{{cite:pcgamer-review}} Examples: the V75 SCAR uses a Volt Battery and the V00 Zeus railgun uses a Volt Cell.{{cite:gamesradar-weapons}}

## MIPS and special ammo behavior

MIPS ammunition is an expensive power ammo type used by certain shotguns and snipers.{{cite:pcgamer-review}} MIPS ammo only stacks up to 15 rounds.{{cite:pcgamer-review}} Examples: the Misriah-2442 shotgun and Longshot sniper both use MIPS ammo.{{cite:gamesradar-weapons}}

## Design and economy notes

Season 2 tuning focuses on ammo economy and clearer weapon roles to make choices more intentional.{{cite:bungie-s2-combat-tuning}} Marathon's weapon and ammo design aims to make close-range tools dangerous and mid-range options hold ground.{{cite:bungie-s2-combat-tuning}}

## See also

- [[weapons|Weapons]]

## Mod slots and chips

Weapons have three or four mod slots for attachments like optics, magazines, barrels, and grips.{{cite:gamesradar-weapons}} Every weapon has a single chip slot reserved for a chip that grants an extra weapon perk.{{cite:gamesradar-weapons}}

## Chips and new chip families

Season 2 adds eight new chips to the game.{{cite:bungie-welcome-season-2}} Examples of Season 2 chips include Brain Freeze, Alarmist, and Scrapyard chips.{{cite:bungie-welcome-season-2}} Bungie described chips as the software upgrades that enable notable weapon behavior changes.{{cite:bungie-s2-combat-tuning}}

## Folding stock

Folding Stocks are a new Season 2 mod family that alter weapon gameplay via a unique action.{{cite:bungie-s2-combat-tuning}} The KKV-9SD and the D54 Battle Pistol are the first weapons to gain Folding Stocks.{{cite:bungie-s2-combat-tuning}} Using the Folding Stock lets players keep it closed for hip-fire benefits or unfold it for ADS benefits.{{cite:bungie-welcome-season-2}}

## Prestige mods and tuning

Prestige weapon mods exist and their drop chances were slightly reduced in Update 1.1.0.1 (June 9, 2026).{{cite:bungie-update-1-1-0-1}} Season 2 adjusted existing Prestige mods such as reducing Full-Auto Selector's rate of fire bonus.{{cite:bungie-s2-combat-tuning}} The KKV-9SD's Prestige mod Flechette Drum heals the player when breaking a hostile's shield.{{cite:bungie-s2-combat-tuning}} Patch notes fixed issues related to specific Prestige mods, including the D54 Battle Pistol's Daredevil Stock (Update 1.1.0.1, June 9, 2026).{{cite:bungie-update-1-1-0-1}}

